      <h1>Evidence After a San Francisco Car Accident: What You Need to Keep</h1>
      <div class="entry-content">
<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> After a San Francisco car accident, the evidence that decides your claim is rarely handed to you: photos of the scene before anything moves, the other driver's and witnesses' contact details, the collision report, your medical records, and a running file of every bill and wage statement the crash touched. Some of it runs on a clock — you have 10 days to file your DMV SR-1, many surveillance systems record over themselves within days, and if a Muni bus, a city pothole, or other public property was involved, you have six months, not two years, to present a claim.</p>
<p>Insurance adjusters don't evaluate crashes. They evaluate files. This is how to build one that survives contact with the adjuster — organized by how fast each piece disappears.</p>
<h2>What Counts as Evidence After a California Car Accident</h2>
<p>Anything that shows what happened, who is responsible, and what it cost you. California Courts' self-help guide to personal injury cases lists the core: photographs of the scene and your injuries, medical records and bills, witness statements, police reports, and proof of lost income. <a href="https://www.selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/civil-lawsuit/personal-injury">See the list on the courts' site</a>.</p>
<p>That list is accurate and a little useless on its own, because the items on it don't expire at the same rate. A photo you didn't take at the scene is gone an hour after the tow truck leaves. A witness's number is gone when they drive off. The lawsuit that uses all of it has two years on the clock. So below, the categories run in order of how fast each one disappears — not how important they are.</p>
<h2>The First Hour: Photograph This Before You Leave the Scene</h2>
<p>Photos are the one evidence you can never recreate, and they are the easiest to get wrong. "Accident photos" in an insurance file are usually two parked cars on a swept street — a picture of the scene, not of the accident.</p>
<p>Take two kinds. Close-ups of every point of contact: scratches, bumper damage, broken glass, deployed airbags, and any visible injury — those prove what broke. Then step back and shoot the whole scene from all four directions, wide enough to catch where the cars came to rest relative to the crosswalk, the signals, the lane lines, and the hill. The close-ups prove the damage; the wide frames prove position, and position is the first thing that changes, because the cars get towed and the skid marks wash off in the next rain. Keep going while it's still there: skid marks and debris before anyone sweeps them, traffic signs and signals, the road surface, weather and lighting, and the other vehicle's plate.</p>
<p>Take them yourself. Photographs count as evidence when the person who took them can say they accurately show the scene — that's you, with your phone, standing there. A blurry frame from a stranger's phone that you can't explain is worth less than nothing.</p>
<p>For everything else in that first hour — checking injuries, calling police, exchanging information — work the full sequence in our guide to <a href="/blog/what-should-you-do-immediately-after-a-car-accident/">what to do immediately after a car accident</a>, then come back here. Evidence is the part that starts where that checklist ends.</p>
<h2>The Police Report — and the 10-Day DMV Deadline</h2>
<p>Two documents, two clocks, and most drivers only know about one of them.</p>
<p><strong>The collision report.</strong> If SFPD responded, the officer's report carries the narrative, the parties, and often a fault finding that the insurance adjuster will quote back at you for the next year. It isn't ready instantly: SFPD asks you to allow at least five business days after the crash before requesting a copy, and requests go through their online portal — <a href="https://www.sanfranciscopolice.org/get-service/request-copy-police-incident-report">request a collision report from SFPD</a> — taking about ten business days to process. Put the request in the week it opens, not the week the insurer demands answers.</p>
<p><strong>The SR-1.</strong> This one is on you, at fault or not. Under California Vehicle Code section 16000, every driver must file an SR-1 with the DMV within 10 days of an accident that injures or kills anyone, or does more than $1,000 in property damage. A police report doesn't cover it, and neither does calling your own insurance company — the DMV is explicit that the SR-1 is required <a href="https://www.dmv.ca.gov/portal/dmv-virtual-office/accident-reporting/">in addition to any report made to police, the CHP, or your insurer</a>. Miss the deadline and your driver's license can be suspended.</p>
<p>The report itself isn't the verdict either. When it's thin or silent — and plenty are — photos, witness statements, and medical records are what prove fault. A report is evidence, not the decision.</p>
<h2>Information From the People Involved</h2>
<p>Collect it at the scene, in your notes app, while they are still standing there: the other driver's name, driver's license number, insurer and policy number, plate, and the vehicle owner's name if it differs from theirs. For witnesses — name, phone, email, and one line on what they saw. People scatter from crash scenes within minutes, and the ones who stopped to help don't come back when you realize you never got a number.</p>
<p>Two don'ts. Don't argue about fault at the scene: you're not qualified yet, nobody is, and "you came out of nowhere" has sunk settlements. And know that California is a comparative-fault state — even sharing blame doesn't end a claim. What the evidence shows about each driver's share is what decides it. We break down how that works in <a href="/blog/what-happens-if-you-were-partially-at-fault-for-an-accident/">what happens if you were partially at fault for an accident</a>.</p>
<h2>Medical Records: Tying the Injury to the Crash</h2>
<p>Your body is a witness that testifies politely. Adrenaline blunts whiplash, torn ligaments, and herniated discs for a day or two, so the crash feels like nothing until Tuesday. Get looked at within the first 72 hours even if you feel fine. It's evidence, not just self-care, for two reasons: the record anchors your injury to the collision date, and adjusters read a gap between the crash and your first doctor's visit as proof you weren't really hurt.</p>
<p>Keep everything treatment generates — ER and doctor records, imaging results, prescriptions, therapy notes, follow-up appointments, every bill — plus what the injury cost at work: pay stubs, employer statements, time-off records, and tax records if you're self-employed. That folder is where the claim gets its number. For the non-economic half, see <a href="/blog/how-pain-and-suffering-damages-are-calculated/">how pain and suffering damages are actually calculated</a>.</p>
<h2>Footage, Dashcams, and the Windows That Close</h2>
<p>San Francisco has more cameras than any street in California: storefronts, apartment lobbies, parking garages, Muni stops, and dashcams on half the cars that watched it happen. All of it runs on a timer — most systems record over old footage automatically, and the window can be as short as a few days.</p>
<p>If you or a witness can point at a camera, get the business or building name and ask that same day for the clip to be saved. A request the afternoon of the crash almost always gets a yes; the same request three weeks later usually gets a shrug. Do the same with other drivers and witnesses: ask whether they have dashcam or phone video before they forget you exist.</p>
<p>Video cuts both ways, and there is a whole discipline to how footage is used for and against a claim — see <a href="/blog/digital-evidence-in-personal-injury-cases-what-you-need-to-know/">digital evidence in personal injury cases</a>. And if the other driver took off, the camera sweep plus the police report is also how a <a href="/blog/what-to-do-after-a-hit-and-run-accident-in-california/">hit-and-run claim</a> actually gets paid.</p>
<h2>Evidence of Your Losses: Bills, Wages, and Property Damage</h2>
<img src="/assets/blog/accident-records-organized.webp" alt="Accident paperwork, car keys, and reading glasses organized on a desk" class="blog-post-image" loading="lazy" />
<p>This is the half people treat as paperwork, and it's the half that sets the number. Repair estimates and invoices, towing and storage bills, rental-car receipts, receipts for the property that got totaled along with the car — laptop, phone, child seat — medical bills, and the wage records above. Photograph your vehicle before the repairs, keep the originals of everything, and file the insurance company's written statements alongside your own. Every dollar you can't prove is a dollar the adjuster won't pay.</p>
<h2>The Deadlines: What Disappears and When</h2>
<p>Put all of this on one page of the same folder:</p>
