<p>That six-month row is the one that blindsides people. If a Muni bus, a city-owned pothole, an SFPD pursuit, or any public property played a part, you must present a written claim to that government entity within six months of the crash — the two-year clock never applies to it, and a claim filed in month seven is dead no matter how good it is. California Courts keeps the current deadlines on <a href="https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/civil-lawsuit/statute-limitations">its statute of limitations page</a>.</p>
<h2>Mistakes That Weaken Your Evidence File</h2>
<p>The file gets damaged more often by its own owner than by the other side:</p>
<ul>
<li>Deleting photos to free up your phone. Keep the originals — don't crop, filter, or overwrite them.</li>
<li>Posting about the crash or your recovery. Anything public ends up in the adjuster's hands, and a smiling photo at a barbecue three weeks post-crash will be argued as proof you were never hurt.</li>
<li>Giving a recorded statement to the other driver's insurer before you've talked to anyone about your rights.</li>
<li>Signing a quick property-damage release that quietly settles your injury claim for nothing.</li>
</ul>
<p>The longer list of what actually sinks claims is in <a href="/blog/common-mistakes-that-can-reduce-your-personal-injury-settlement/">common mistakes that reduce your personal injury settlement</a>.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<h3>What is the most important evidence after a car accident?</h3>
<p>Photos of the scene before anything moves, taken by you. They show where the vehicles came to rest, the road and signal conditions, the damage, and any visible injury — and unlike a witness memory or a towed car, they cannot be recreated afterward. Medical records that tie your injuries to the crash date come second.</p>
<h3>How do I get a copy of my San Francisco collision report?</h3>
<p>Request it online through the San Francisco Police Department's records portal. SFPD asks you to wait at least five business days after the crash so the report can be reviewed and approved for release, and requests take about ten business days to process — so put the request in as soon as it opens, not when the insurance company demands answers.</p>
<h3>Do I have to file an SR-1 if the police already made a report?</h3>
<p>Yes. Under California Vehicle Code section 16000, every driver must file an SR-1 with the DMV within 10 days of an accident involving any injury or death, or more than $1,000 in property damage, regardless of who was at fault. The DMV is explicit that the SR-1 is required in addition to any report made to police, the CHP, or your insurance company.</p>
<h3>How long do I have to file a claim after a California car accident?</h3>
<p>Two years from the date of injury for most personal injury lawsuits, under Code of Civil Procedure section 335.1, and three years for property damage under section 338. If a government entity was involved — a Muni bus, a city pothole, public property — you must present a written claim within six months of the crash under Government Code section 911.2, and that shorter deadline is the one that catches people out.</p>
<h3>How long does surveillance footage last after an accident?</h3>
<p>There is no fixed rule — most systems record over old footage automatically, and the window can be as short as a few days or as long as a few weeks. If you can see a camera on a storefront, lobby, garage, or Muni stop, get the business name and ask them to save that day's clip. A request the same day almost always works; the same request a few weeks later usually does not.</p>
<h3>Can social media be used against my claim?</h3>
<p>Yes. Posts, photos, and comments get pulled out of context and handed to the adjuster or the defense — one smiling photo at a family event can be argued as proof your injuries were exaggerated. Say nothing about the crash or your recovery publicly, and remember that deleting content after a claim has started can create its own problem.</p>
